{% extends "pages/_guide.jinja" %}
{% set guide_slug = 'feature-files' %}
{% set thesis = 'A feature file is the part of the suite everyone reads. Scenarios, Background, Outlines, tables, docstrings, tags, and other languages, with the Rust each one needs.' %}
{% set sections = [
  {'id': 'scenario', 'label': 'One scenario'},
  {'id': 'background', 'label': 'Background, tables, docstrings'},
  {'id': 'outline', 'label': 'Scenario Outlines'},
  {'id': 'tags', 'label': 'Tags'},
  {'id': 'languages', 'label': 'Other languages'},
  {'id': 'fine-print', 'label': 'Fine print'},
] %}
{% block guide %}
          <section id="scenario" aria-labelledby="scenario-title">
            <h2 id="scenario-title">One scenario</h2>
            <p>
              A file starts with <code>Feature:</code> and holds one or more
              scenarios. Each scenario is a list of steps, and each step starts
              with a keyword: <code>Given</code> for the starting state,
              <code>When</code> for the action, <code>Then</code> for the
              result. <code>And</code> and <code>But</code> continue whichever
              keyword came before.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/lantern.feature') }}
            <p>
              Write steps about what someone could observe, not about the code
              that makes it happen. “The lantern arrives upright” survives a
              rewrite of the trolley; “<code>upright</code> is true” does not.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="background" aria-labelledby="background-title">
            <h2 id="background-title">Background, tables, and docstrings</h2>
            <p>
              A <code>Background</code> runs before every scenario in the file.
              A data table follows a step as rows between pipes; a docstring
              follows a step between triple quotes. Both belong to the step
              above them.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/hamper.feature') }}
            <p>
              A step receives its table through a <code>#[datatable]</code>
              parameter and its docstring through a parameter named
              <code>docstring</code>. <code>Rows&lt;Item&gt;</code> parses each
              row into a struct that derives <code>DataTableRow</code>, matching
              columns to fields by the header; <code>#[datatable(truthy)]</code>
              reads “yes” and “no” as booleans.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/hamper.rs') }}
            {% call ui.note('A table can stay untyped') %}
            <p>
              <code>Vec&lt;Vec&lt;String&gt;&gt;</code> takes the table as it
              is written, header row and all. Reach for <code>Rows&lt;T&gt;</code>
              once the steps start parsing cells by hand.
            </p>
            {% endcall %}
          </section>

          <section id="outline" aria-labelledby="outline-title">
            <h2 id="outline-title">Scenario Outlines</h2>
            <p>
              An Outline is a scenario with placeholders in angle brackets and
              an <code>Examples</code> table. Each row becomes a test case, and
              each <code>&lt;placeholder&gt;</code> is replaced by that row’s
              value before any step is matched, so the steps see
              <code>a trolley carrying 2 lanterns</code>.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/counting.feature') }}
            <p>
              The rows run as <code>counting_lanterns::case_1</code>,
              <code>case_2</code>, and <code>case_3</code>. The Rust is on the
              <a href="/rstest-bdd/getting-started/#repeat">getting started</a>
              page.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="tags" aria-labelledby="tags-title">
            <h2 id="tags-title">Tags</h2>
            <p>
              A tag is a word starting with <code>@</code>, on the line above a
              feature, a scenario, or an Examples table. Tags select which
              scenarios become tests, and <code>@allow_skipped</code> marks a
              skip as expected.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/auto/weather.feature') }}
            <p>
              Selection happens when the test compiles, so a scenario a tag
              expression leaves out is not a test at all rather than an
              ignored one. The expressions are in
              <a href="/rstest-bdd/guides/binding-and-filtering/#tags">bind and filter scenarios</a>.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="languages" aria-labelledby="languages-title">
            <h2 id="languages-title">Other languages</h2>
            <p>
              A <code># language:</code> line at the top of the file switches
              the keywords to any language Gherkin knows. Nothing else needs
              configuring; the step patterns are written in the same language
              as the steps.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/pique_nique.feature') }}
            {{ ui.sample('tests/pique_nique.rs') }}
            <p>
              The repository’s <code>japanese-ledger</code> example does the
              same in Japanese, with a <code>背景</code> (Background) and
              typed placeholders.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="fine-print" aria-labelledby="fine-print-title">
            <h2 id="fine-print-title">Fine print</h2>
            {% call ui.note('What the parser does not do', 'caveat') %}
            <p>
              The <code>*</code> step keyword is parsed but never matched to a
              step definition. Tags on an Examples table select rows for
              compilation, but <code>@allow_skipped</code> is read from the
              feature and the scenario only.
            </p>
            {% endcall %}
          </section>
{% endblock %}
